 "captions": {
  "script": "This glacier is bleeding. And nobody is hurt. Glitch Diary, entry six. Blood Falls, Taylor Glacier, Antarctica. In nineteen eleven, explorers saw red streaks pouring out of the white ice, and they blamed red algae. But that's not the strange part. The water comes out clear. It's brine, saltier than the sea, so salty it stays liquid below freezing. The moment it touches the air, its iron rusts. Blood red. And there's something even stranger. Under the ice, scientists found microbes living with no light and no oxygen, sealed away for about one and a half million years. In twenty twenty-three, they finally caught the red itself: iron nanospheres, a hundred times smaller than a red blood cell. Could life hide like this on Mars? Tell me in the comments. Follow for the next entry of my glitch diary. Because this glacier is still bleeding...",
